Subject: [PATCH] server: idle-sleep with wake reload (v0.17 series 0005/0006/0007 ported to v0.18)

Port of the local v0.17 patches 0005/0006/0007 onto v0.18.0-llamacpp-rc1.

Apply AFTER 0008 and 0009: on v0.18 the guard hunks live in
tools/llama-kvmem-server.cpp, the same file this restructure rewrites.

v0.18 moved the entire load path into main() (model, context, lanes,
conversation arming) and introduced ServerState/LlamaEngineState with execution
scopes, so the three concerns no longer split into independent hunks. This one
patch carries all of them:

  0005 --sleep-idle-seconds : unload model/context/vision after N seconds without
                              a request, reload it on the next one.
  0006 conversation stores  : drop the conversation table, host stores and disk
                              snapshots on unload so no dangling handle survives
                              the wake (the next request starts cold).
  0007 wake retry           : retry the reload for up to 60 s (5 s steps) because
                              other CUDA processes keep their VRAM until their own
                              idle timer fires, and free a half-built runtime
                              before retrying.

Two v0.18-specific pitfalls are handled here; neither exists in the v0.17 series:

  * main() holds `startup_execution` (a kvmem_execution_scope) for the whole
    startup, so every adapter query during startup sees the execution state. The
    wake path is an HTTP worker thread and must establish that scope itself,
    otherwise llama_kvmem_store_swap_supported() answers for an empty execution
    state, the conversation arming fails and the reload dies half way.
  * the idle monitor and the request path must be serialized: the monitor holds
    sleep_mu while it unloads, and the request takes the same lock before it
    counts itself in. Without that an arriving request reads st.ctx/st.vision
    while the monitor frees them (SIGSEGV on the second idle cycle).

Verified on the RTX 5060 Ti with the 27B IQ3_S: 4 idle/wake cycles (45 s idle,
including two ~58k-token requests) - VRAM 15050 -> 1074 MiB on unload, wake in
~5 s with a correct completion, process alive after every cycle, no log errors.

